WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.940
SPEAKER: These days, just about
every person in your business

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:06.190
asks questions about your data.

00:00:06.190 --> 00:00:09.070
And the faster they can get
answers to those questions,

00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:12.340
the more efficiently
they can do their jobs.

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:14.920
Not only can
optimizing SQL queries

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:17.540
keep all your data
consumers happy,

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:20.330
but it can also save you money.

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:23.380
In this video, we're going to
talk through some strategies

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:26.306
for optimizing your
BigQuery queries.

00:00:26.306 --> 00:00:29.778
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:36.990
In our last episode, we
covered query execution,

00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:39.290
including several
tools and concepts

00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:42.500
that provide the foundation
for many of the optimization

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:45.440
techniques we're
highlighting today.

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:47.780
If you haven't already,
I highly recommend

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:51.770
taking a pause to watch it
before diving into this one.

00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:52.490
All right.

00:00:52.490 --> 00:00:55.010
Onto the strategies.

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:57.980
One of the most straightforward
ways to optimize a query

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:00.410
is to make sure that you
limit the number of bytes

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:02.540
it needs to scan.

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:04.160
The first thing
you want to do is

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:06.950
ensure you are only selecting
the columns that you really

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:07.940
need.

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:10.610
That means no SELECT *.

00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:13.130
Since BigQuery uses
columnar storage,

00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:15.650
the data scanned is
proportional to the number

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:18.240
of columns used in the query.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:21.440
If you want to take a peek at
the first few rows of the data

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:23.600
to get a feel for what
you're working with,

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:27.050
use the Preview Table
feature instead.

00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:29.590
The next thing you can do
is leverage partitioning

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:32.050
and clustering to
automatically prune out

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:34.390
data you don't
need in your query,

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:38.530
like we talked about in our
video on BigQuery storage.

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:41.260
This includes leveraging
filters or WHERE

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:43.870
clauses on columns
used to partition

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:46.640
or cluster the table itself.

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:47.140
All right.

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:49.810
So you went over some
strategies for minimizing

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:51.850
the data scanned in the query.

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:55.810
The next thing we can look
closer at is aggregations.

00:01:55.810 --> 00:01:59.140
To understand how to
optimize aggregation queries,

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:01.180
let's look at how
BigQuery actually

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:05.310
executes a query that
uses a GROUP BY clause.

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:08.699
First, the data is read
from our storage system,

00:02:08.699 --> 00:02:12.240
and GROUP BYs are done
on individual slots.

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:14.520
Then, the results
from each slot are

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:17.610
bucketed using a hash
function, such that data

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:20.760
from the same key ends
up on the same slot.

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:24.090
We often refer to
this as shuffling.

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:27.960
So in this query, all partial
aggregations for the same wiki

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:31.110
title end up in the same slot.

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:34.950
And we can see the shuffle step
happening in the second stage

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:37.680
of the execution details.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:40.680
Further aggregation occurs,
and the final result

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:43.170
is then passed along
to the last slot,

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:45.810
for which limits the results.

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:49.050
Since this requires a few
different aggregation steps,

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:51.870
GROUP BY queries can be costly.

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:53.760
For this reason,
you want to ensure

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:57.880
that you aggregate as late
and as seldom as possible.

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:01.320
This can come into play for
queries that have sub-SELECTs.

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:03.720
You'll want to move your
aggregation functions up

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:07.830
to the top to avoid multiple
GROUP BY executions.

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:10.110
But there is an exception.

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:12.690
If a table can be
reduced drastically

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:16.140
by aggregating in
preparation for being joined,

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:18.750
then aggregate it early.

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:21.630
Another thing you can do
to avoid using GROUP BYs

00:03:21.630 --> 00:03:24.100
is to nest repeated data.

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:27.000
For example, you might
have a transactions table

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.770
that has information
about the order,

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:32.400
including the items
that were purchased.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:35.310
If you use flat
denormalization, you

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:38.520
would have one row
for each ordered item.

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:42.270
Instead, you can create a
repeated column that contains

00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:45.000
a list of all the items.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:48.960
Now if you want to calculate the
number of items in each order,

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:54.110
you can use ARRAY_LENGTH and
avoid doing any GROUP BYs.

00:03:54.110 --> 00:03:54.610
Great.

00:03:54.610 --> 00:03:57.810
So now you have some
tactics for aggregations.

00:03:57.810 --> 00:03:59.940
But what about JOINs?

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:02.820
Let's begin by looking
at how BigQuery executes

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:04.740
different types of JOINs.

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:07.440
If you join two large
tables together,

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:12.180
BigQuery favors what's called
a hash JOIN, or a shuffle JOIN.

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:14.340
Similar to the
aggregation we discussed

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:17.430
before, BigQuery
uses a hash function

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:20.459
to shuffle both the left
and the right tables

00:04:20.459 --> 00:04:23.760
so that the matching keys
end up on the same worker,

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:26.910
and then the data can
be joined locally.

00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:28.980
On the other hand,
if one of your tables

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:31.200
is small enough
to fit in memory,

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:35.100
it's not necessary to shuffle
large amounts of data.

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:38.070
In a small JOIN,
or broadcast JOIN,

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:40.350
BigQuery simply
sends the small table

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:45.120
to every single node that's
processing the larger table.

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:48.750
Now that you have a feel for
how BigQuery executes joins,

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:50.850
let's consider how
you can make queries

00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:54.840
that join multiple tables
as efficient as possible.

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:57.660
First off, you want to make
sure you place the largest

00:04:57.660 --> 00:05:01.290
table first in the JOIN query,
followed by the smallest,

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:04.020
and then by decreasing size.

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:08.670
Next, WHERE clauses should be
executed as soon as possible so

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:10.380
that the slots for
forming the JOIN

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:13.660
are working with the
least amount of data.

00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:17.580
You also want to make sure we're
using filters on both tables,

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:19.770
like in this example here.

00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:22.350
This way, you're ensuring
data you don't need

00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:26.500
is being eliminated from
both sides of the JOIN.

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:29.410
Finally, you can cluster
or partition tables

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:33.280
based on common JOIN keys,
because less data is scanned

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:35.500
and the query will
execute faster.

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:37.990
And actually, clustering
improves the efficiency

00:05:37.990 --> 00:05:39.850
even more than partitioning.

00:05:39.850 --> 00:05:42.040
So keep this in mind
when deciding on how

00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:44.110
to structure your tables.

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:47.140
One last way to optimize
queries is by tweaking the way

00:05:47.140 --> 00:05:49.690
you do filtering and ordering.

00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:52.090
Something you may not
realize is that when

00:05:52.090 --> 00:05:54.190
you are using a WHERE
clause, BigQuery

00:05:54.190 --> 00:05:58.780
assumes that you have chosen
the best order for your filters,

00:05:58.780 --> 00:06:00.850
meaning the first part
of your WHERE clause

00:06:00.850 --> 00:06:03.910
should always contain a filter
that will eliminate the most

00:06:03.910 --> 00:06:06.260
data.

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:08.950
Next, if you're using
an ORDER BY statement,

00:06:08.950 --> 00:06:12.590
you may run into a
Resources Exceeded error.

00:06:12.590 --> 00:06:15.190
This is because the final
sorting for your query

00:06:15.190 --> 00:06:17.800
must be done on a single slot.

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:21.010
And if you're attempting to
order a very large result set,

00:06:21.010 --> 00:06:23.230
the final sorting can
overwhelm the slot

00:06:23.230 --> 00:06:26.000
that's processing the data.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:29.930
One way you can solve this is by
making sure that you use LIMIT,

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:33.370
so that the result set
is easier to manage.

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:34.090
Phew!

00:06:34.090 --> 00:06:37.760
OK, we were able to cover
a lot of great techniques

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:40.130
to make your queries
more performant.

